A||N||57||Verticalspeed||*** V-8 Stuff Only *** Poll||||5||
Z||000000||Verticalspeed||01-02-2001||12:21 AM||||Do you agree with the following comments in regards to a V-8 conversion. This was posted by another site that provides a different engine conversion for the fiero.<BR><B>quote</B>:<P>The reasons are:<P>1 - Weight - The large increase in weight degrades handling greatly<P>2 - Reliability - Not in the engine but in the transmission.  No Fiero transmission is designed for the torque delivered by the small block.  The Getrag 5 speed & Muncie 4 Speed are designed for about 200 ft, pounds of torque.  The Izuzu 5 speed used on the Iron Duke 4 Cylinder is good for only about 120 Ft. pounds of torque....<P>3 - Size - Not only the subframe but the chassis needs major modification to fit a small block.  Most installations we have seen use solid mounts to keep it from moving.<P>4 - Emissions - Our kits use current transverse powertrains that meet all current regulations and we show how to connect all emission equipment.  <P>5 - Cooling - Our engine selections normally give over 30 mpg. on the highway and over 20 mpg. in town.  Better economy than the 2.8 V6, therefore generating less waste heat that the cooling system needs to get rid of.    <P> <P>  <P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Oreif||01-02-2001||01:48 AM||||I dis-agree with all the above. A few of our club members have V-8 Fieros and all are using Fiero transmissions. None have failed.<BR>There is only 80lbs difference between the 2.8L and a SBC. There is no major/structural changes required for a V-8 install. The frame is not modified. Depending on engine type a couple of access holes need to be cut. As for emissions, those in the club that have them pass testing without any problems. ("They show YOU how to hook it up???") Just because 1 engine gets more MPG than another does not mean the engine runs cooler. The ability of the cooling system (capacity) is what helps maintain the engine temps. Yes if the cooling system is set up properly MPG and performance increase but it has nothing to do "generating less heat waste" The cylinder head temps of many different engines with proper cooling systems would be the same. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||stimpy||01-02-2001||02:27 AM||||Am I correct that these statements come off of ACE website? While I concur that a s/c 3.8 Fiero makes for one bad-assed ride, I would do a little more research into customer satisfaction regarding the installer before coughing up funds. Look to Chris West to do it right if that is what you want.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Standard||01-02-2001||04:24 AM||||No tranny put in the Fiero was ever designed to handle the torque put out by the 3.8 series II s/c, either... No tranny will last long if you do a lot of drop-clutch starts and power shifting.  It's just a matter of driving the car resposibly.. although I have a hard time doing that with a worn 2.8 behind me.. another 100 hp and lb/ft would be interesting, to say the least  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||01-02-2001||09:17 AM||||Yes, that came from the ACE website.<BR>Let's look at some other scatterbrained goodies and things that make you go "Hmmm..." from the same location:<BR>     They cite the SC 3800 as putting out 289 ftlbs of torque. The Northstar puts out 295 ft lbs. They caution that a manual transmission must be driven with extreme care behind a Northstar, but give no such cautions about the 3.8. I think they're posting propaganda to turn people away from Northstar conversions because such conversions are difficult and people who will have them done are rare. I think that they offer the conversion as a status symbol for their business and little or nothing more.<BR>     They post dire warnings that SBC's destroy Getrags, but when pressed for details admit that they've only heard of it, and never actually seen one.<BR>     Their site is full of typos and is poorly written.<BR>     And of course the misinformation that started thsi thread.<BR>     I would never buy anything from them. Much of the information on their site is worse than worthless because it contains a grain of truth but is misleading. I think that they do this on purpose out of a bias driven by commercialism.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||stimpy||01-02-2001||10:18 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Standard:<BR><B>No tranny put in the Fiero was ever designed to handle the torque put out by the 3.8 series II s/c, either...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>But the difference is that there is a "matched" tranny available that drops in, the 4T60E.  GM never made a FWD package involving a SBC engine.  <BR>Again, I think the s/c 3.8 provides plenty adequate torque and hp to be a ripsnorting powerplant. But I really do think that one should be cautious about sending their car to Colorado. I have seen the results of an ACE swap very up close and personal. Not impressive.<BR>Look under the hood of any ACE conversion. You will see gouges out of the underside of the hood right above the alternator. This is because there is ZERO provision for a torque strut (dogbone) thereby driving the alternator pulley into the decklid upon acceleration. This stress is also shared by motor mounts,exhaust system, and tranny.<BR>This is just one example of under engineering with an ACE conversion. I could list a couple more off the top of my head. And this doesn't bring into play their poor business ethic.  No sir, I don't like it!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Verticalspeed||01-02-2001||12:04 PM||||<B>by comparison, a v8 provides: </B><P>* Bat out of hell acceleration after 1st gear<BR>* No fear from imports or stock domestics<BR>* Decent 1/4 mile runs, lower 13's high 12's<BR>* Easy maintenance and/or replacement<BR>* Easy wiring<BR>* Top end performance with the right ignition<BR>* You can challenge most cars without fear<P><B> BUT</B>:<P>* No frequent clutch dumps from 3k rpms!<BR>* No extreme power shifting<BR>* No burnouts<P><B>And</B><BR>The V-8 conversion is as good as the condition the car was in with the 2.5-2.8 in it. As good as the installer. A clean, solid suspension vehicle is preferred before dumping a v8 powerhouse into it.<P><B>For kickass power</B>:<BR>383 stroker<BR>LS1<BR>ZZ4<BR>LT1<P>You can run with the big boys with one of the above set ups.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black88GT||01-02-2001||12:30 PM||||I heard the getrag was rated up to 250 lb. ft. of torque?<P>I figure once I get over that I will just get one beefed up, or get an Isuzu beefed up, Isuzu's are a lot cheaper and easier to find, so if you blow it it's not a big loss.<P>About the Qtr. mile times, I haven't seen any SBC V8 owner post any time slips so those high 12s low 13s statement is a guess.  <P>Easy maintenance, I'm not so sure about that either.  Things do look pretty crammed in the engine compartment.  I have never had a V8 so that is just IMHO.<P>The thing about the V8 is the extra weight.  The Fiero is heavy enough, it doesn't need any more weight.  You can get the same power out of a turbo setup cheaper and easier. Look at Steve Hamm for example, he is selling his turbo setup, with a reinforced 2.8 he put out 322 Hp to the crank and something like 375 lb. ft. of torque at 10 psi.  Imagine that setup on a built up 3.4, much cheaper then any V8 install, and you can probably do it yourself in a lot less time.  A guy in my club dynoed his ZZ4 Fiero at 250 Rwhp.  That's great, but considering all the money he spent to do that, it's not really that great at all.  If you took all the money he paid for labor alone and dumped it into a good turbo setup you could make that with the proper tuning.<P>all IMHO of course<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000008||BBBAD GT||01-02-2001||01:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR> Look at Steve Hamm for example, he is selling his turbo setup, with a reinforced 2.8 he put out 322 Hp to the crank and something like 375 lb. ft. of torque at 10 psi.  Imagine that setup on a built up 3.4, much cheaper then any V8 install, and you can probably do it yourself in a lot less time.  A guy in my club dynoed his ZZ4 Fiero at 250 Rwhp.  That's great, but considering all the money he spent to do that, it's not really that great at all.  If you took all the money he paid for labor alone and dumped it into a good turbo setup you could make that with the proper tuning.<BR>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I hope you don't think Steve Hamn just plunked a turbo on the 2.8 and got those HP numbers.  As he states, his motor is 'completely' built up, sorta adds to the price of the turbo conversion.  <P>Whatever you put behind a Fiero, you will ultimately be at the chassis mercy, because if you can't get that power to the ground effectively, then all that HP won't do you any good.  <P><P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">     <P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Slammed Fiero||01-02-2001||03:10 PM||||The chassis of a fiero is built like a TANK , I compare the weight of the doors to that of my 79 cadillac. I got in a rear end accident , where I hit a van (crv thingie) it made the crv in operable , I then proceeded (after the accident report) to drive my fiero home 3 hours in toronto traffic . A fiero chassis can handle a lot of power. there overbuilt , much like a Fiat x19<P>2nd. fiero's weigh a lot ...granted , for the size of them. But look at other supercars in todays market , porsche's , corvettes and vipers are all 3100lbs plus. V8 fiero with aluminum heads and other aluminum components is in the neighbourhood of 2900lbs (I had my 86 GT weighed in at 2780lbs at the last autocross with spare tire and full tank o gas) So as for the v8 giving you adverse handling conditions , it's not the added 150lbs or so that archie quotes ,it's the inherient design of the suspension. If you can make a fiero work suspension wise , adding a v8 won't do much. A mid engine car is the least likley to be affected by a bigger motor. Fiero's if there to be used as serious corner carvers need suspension work regaurdless of motor. other than a bit of hobby horsing upon braking and acceleration I can't see the V8 doing "adverse" things to the suspension. <P>As for the ZZ4 with 250rear wheel Hp ..I would bet it has a TPi on it. The more I read about ZZ4's and stock-ish TPi's the more I hear there low on power. Put a carb on that motor ..may not be as fancy , but the motor will put out it's factory spec #'s. YEah a turbo is great , but they also have there downfalls.. Reliability wise ..gimmie a v8 any day. there's a reason all the taxi's in the world are chev caprices.  Sure a turbo kit for a fiero is around 3 grand , a v8 archie kit is about the same. But I have to go get a motor, GM perf parts crate motor , plus a used TPI (ebay $250) all in all I could quote prices for expensive turbo parts such as delta gates , blow off vales , ball bearing turbo's , intercoolers ...ect. The point is there in the same neighbourhood price wise for equal performance. There are guys out there I have huge respect for like Dennis L who built his own turbo , but he's an obvious exception to the case. Turbo's are not as reliable as naturally aspirated motors , turbo seals , coaxing of the oil cooled turbo's , bearings , housings cracking ...ect..ect. all make them less reliable than a V8 by far. A v8 may not be high tech , but there is a reason you see 100's of punks in you cities in 5.0ltr mustangs and camaro's..there common , reliable and easy to fix, yet they create gobs of torque at low RPM's<P>yes the tranny's reliability is a question to be pondered. But like anything , with abuse it will break. I don't believe you need a clutch dump of 3000rpm in a v8 fiero to hook up. thats insanity. The best launch for most c4/c5 vettes is 12-1600 rpm , there's enough torque there to move you. keep in mind the Fiero has more traction due to it's engine placement.<P>Just my thoughts.. I have had a merkur with the 2.3ltr turbo, I have relaced turbo's due to all sorts or reasons. cracked hgousings , meltdown ect. <P>Just a matter of taste I guess.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...||||reg||1
Z||000010||Verticalspeed||01-02-2001||03:10 PM|||| <BR>I doubt any turbo fiero 2.8 engine can outdo the torque (w/o a turbo) of a high performance small block. <BR>The fiero is not a heavy car. 2,850 lbs with a v8 in it. By comparison with other v8 cars this is not heavy at all. They are all pushing over 3,200-4,000 lbs.<BR>A v8 conversion is not expensive. and the engine is not crammed in there as you stated.<BR>I have plenty of room to work on mine. I like it better than my 88 GT with all those sensors that go bad, vacuum lines etc and nobody (shops) wants to work on the car and send to the dealer with novice fiero mechanics that have no clue how to fix the car.<BR>The only complex thing about a v8 swap is when you get into TPI, LS1 etc. They require more work and are more expensive. For a simple carburated 300hp sbc engine, it's a cinch. You can convert your 2.8 with a v8 with less than $1,000 for conversion parts, another $1,000 for the engine, and another $800 or so for misc. items. All under $3,500.<BR>Unless you live in CA where you need a few extra things for smog testing.<BR>All this other stuff, 3.1, 3.4, 3.8, turbo 2.8 does not appeal to me. I would only settle with a caddy northstar conversion or a v8. But that's me. I like big boys power.<BR>Otherwise I would buy a honda. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Verticalspeed||01-02-2001||03:27 PM|||| Turbos suck. They are expensive to fix and you have to spool them up before they really do anything for the car. I have seen a big turbo on a slant nose porsche with a v8 in it. 10.5 second car at the track. nice set up too.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Will||01-02-2001||04:53 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Verticalspeed:<BR><B> <BR>I doubt any turbo fiero 2.8 engine can outdo the torque (w/o a turbo) of a high performance small block. <BR>&lt;snip&gt;<BR>All this other stuff, 3.1, 3.4, 3.8, turbo 2.8 does not appeal to me. I would only settle with a caddy northstar conversion or a v8. But that's me. I like big boys power.<BR>Otherwise I would buy a honda. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hehe...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> If I built a 2.8 with 7:1 static compression and stacked 30 psi of boost on it, I think it would put out more torque than any streetable NA SBC.<BR>BTW, a Northstar IS a V8  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I like turbos. They are the proverbial replacement for displacement. In a recent issue of Popular Hot Rodding (I think) there was an article about a turbo Impala SS which put out almost 1,000 HP. A streetable NA engine putting out that much power would need 7-800 cubic inches, and would not come close to the 383 LT1's gas mileage.<BR>A well set up turbo system will only have appreciable lag at low RPM. Take the new Prosche 911 Turbo and numerous Saabs as examples. However, when the turbo is too big for the engine there will be a great deal of lag at all but the highest RPM. Single turbo converted Supras have this problem. They make crazy HP once the turbo spools, but it takes a while to do so.<BR>I agree that an Audi TT or 911 turbo may be expensive to fix, but is a T-bird Turbo Coupe? A Plymouth Laser? Dodge Daytona? Chrsyler Le Baron Turbo? Turbo Sunbird? Dennis Lagrua got his hands on a good running turbo without paying much.<BR>There are powerful Hondas out there too. Turbo Civics are lighter than Fieros and can have 240 or more wheel HP.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Will||01-02-2001||05:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>I heard the getrag was rated up to 250 lb. ft. of torque?<P>I figure once I get over that I will just get one beefed up, or get an Isuzu beefed up, Isuzu's are a lot cheaper and easier to find, so if you blow it it's not a big loss.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The 282 Getrag used in our cars is supposedly rated for 200 ftlbs, while the 284 used behind the 3.4 TDC V6 is said to be rated for 250 ftlbs. In the Getrag's case, rating doesn't mean much. GM wanted to use this transmission forever (it's been 14 years and there's no replacement in sight) so they got it right before it ever went into production; thus it isvery conservatively rated. Compare that to the VW 02A in which third gear is regulary broken by STOCK VR6's with limited slip diffs and racing slicks.<P>How do you plan to "beef up" a manual transaxle?||||reg||1
Z||000014||Slammed Fiero||01-02-2001||05:02 PM||||Verticalspeed wrote::<P>Turbos suck. They are expensive to fix and you have to spool them up before they really do anything for the car. I have seen a big turbo on a slant nose porsche with a v8 in it. 10.5 second car at the track. nice set up too.<P><BR>..A big turbo , on a slant nose porsche witha v8 in it... WTF??  Only production V8 porsche is a 928 , there not considered slant nose cars ...911's are (unless were talking about a rod simpson hybird V8 911 bastardization). Comparing the engineering that goes into a 911 turbo , to a fiero turbo is apples and oranges. Turbo 911's have very progressive power...almost like there is no turbo SEAMLESS. In any case , Turbo's do not suck , compared to a v8 swap in a fiero , the power isn't there for the money. As for the issue of spooling up the turbo , depends on so many different factors . Some cars like supras (TT) use a smaller turbo for low end power , then switch to a larger  one for higher speeds. Check out Eddie Bello's artice in the may 2000 issue of turbo , this guy was driving his 9 sec turbo porsche 1500 miles from his shop in New York to Flordia ...winning , and driving it home!! Turbo's allow small displacement motors to create gobs of power. <P>TpI and LS1 are 2 different things , TPI is relatively easy injection system, just look at archies page he basically outlines what is to be done when swapping to a TPI. LS1 is another story ..and one I haven't read up on. I have had the TPI off of our Vette and a friends camaro ..it wasn't difficult at all. the LS1 is vastly different than a small block. I wish archie would let us know the procedures involved in the swap , but I totally understand why he is keeping far away from this one. perhaps that would be a good thread.<P>What it all comes down to is driving habits/ wants/needs. Emmission testing is an issue , even here in Canada , it won't be long before it is an issue everywhere. But the aftermarket industry is adderessing this Before you go saying turbo's suck perhaps you should read up on them. I have seen GEO metro's running the twin cam 1.3ltr motor with a turbo turning 11's in the 1/4. (motor probably didn't last long)Fascinating to say the least. But for the average joe like all of us , affordable power comes from 8cyls.<P>how does a V8 add no weight to a fiero?? My car weighed in a 2850 with a rock stock v6. I think it would tip the scales at around 3000lbs or a bit less depeding on year and tranny used (auto manual , auto 4spd , auto 3spd) In comparison c4 vette weighs 3300lbs with 240hp L98 and runs the 1/4 in 14.02. I think it's safe to say a Fiero with the identical motor is a safe low 13 high 12 sec car. Given the weight is 300lbs less , traction is better and the highest fiero gear ratio available is 3.31 (I believe)<P>I used car test and got 12.6 in the 1/4 with a 2950lbs fiero running a zz4 , lowered to 3.0" clutch dump at 1200rpm<P><BR>This thread should have been stopped at the fact that ace obviously wrote that to sell their kits..which is fine, I believe everything I read on the internet...don't you??<P>Different strokes for different folks.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...||||reg||1
Z||000015||Verticalspeed||01-03-2001||03:53 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/350CHEVY.JPG"> Here’s the skinny on the small block Chevy: it’s easy to work on, performance bits are inexpensive and plentiful, and there’s 45 years of R&D to fall back on. It eagerly adopts to just about any performance modification you can imagine, whether it’s carbureted or injected. Stroke it for torque. Destroke it for revs. Add a supercharger, nitrous, or turbos. Whatever floats your boat. <P>My wagon may be just a brick, but a surprising number of the quickest high-end street legal (and streetable) cars I’ve seen and covered rely on that wheezer V8 for motorvation. Warren Mosler’s slippery, mid-engine Raptor took the One Lap of America title in 1999—his second in three years—kitted up with a 450 horse stroker small block, a Porsche 5-speed and a stock Corvette suspension. Along with A/C, power windows, airbags, and a car phone. He drives it to work.<P>Then there’s the Ultima GTR, the AWD British "kit car" that pushes the 3 second barrier from ought to 60 and turns 11s in the quarter with a stock ‘Vette LS1. You wanna talk top speed? Callaway’s twin-turbo, 900 horse Sledgehammer Corvette dialed in at 254.76 mph in 1989 running a 350 cube Chevy. Ridiculously expensive? Of course it is. They all are, but no more so than any other Warp-Speed supercar. <P>Still, the best part about the SBC is the fact that just about anyone can shake out their sofa and scrape together enough dimes and quarters to build a carbureted 350 that makes 300 horse at the flywheel. Try bumping out 300 ponies from your VTEC or VR6 for small change—or go 255 mph for any amount of money.<P>Anyone who doesn’t agree that the 350 Chevy is the greatest invention in the history of mankind is encouraged to drop me a line.<P>||||reg||12
Z||000016||1FST2M6||01-03-2001||10:12 AM||||geez vert.. i feel like i've just been read victory speech! <P>all hail the SBC!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I agree with ya!||||reg||1
Z||000017||Black88GT||01-03-2001||10:27 AM||||Vertical, I clearly stated that it was just IMHO that is seemed crammed in there.  Somewhere on here I remember a V8 owner said there was an inch between the trunk and the V8 valve cover.  That sounds pretty crammed to me, thats where I based that opinion.  Again, that is an OPINION of mine, not a fact.  <P>I totally agree with Jonathan's post.  If I were going for reliability, a SBC would be it any day over a turbocharger.  BUT, in a Fiero that V8 looks a little crammed so I would imagine that if in the event something would go wrong it would be tough to fix,  then again some people say that its crammed with a V6 in there so I guess thats a moot point.<P>Also vertical, email Steve Hamm about his turbo setup, thats where I got those numbers from.  He is selling it now for only 2500.  <BR>Where did you get those V8 conversion numbers from?  What kind of motor are you getting for 1,000 bucks (305 = 180 Hp?)?  I have heard that while you can try to piece together Archie's kit for cheaper, it's such a pain in the butt that its better to just buy the kit.<P>well gotta run now I have to edit this later<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000018||Oreif||01-03-2001||10:58 AM||||I have seen quite a few V-8 converted Fiero's. With a SBC V-8 you can see the ground from the top.(actually hard to do with the stock 2.8L) A Northstar V-8 seems to be the engine that looks "crammed". A 2.8 with turbo also looks crammed due to the piping and the turbo. As for power vs $$'s. V-8's have a large advantage due to availability of performance parts and the cost. I have yet to see ANY autoparts store not carry SBC performance parts. Even Kmart has performance parts (ignition and distributor parts)! I could go to Trak auto today and get a performace manifold (Holley or Edlebrock) for a SBC and spend less than a 4-cyl performance manifold which Trak doesn't carry. Besides what would you rather listen to, the high-pitched whine of a 4/6-cyl turbo or the rumble of a V-8 exhaust and the sound of a 4bbl carb sucking 850cfm of air!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000019||stimpy||01-03-2001||11:12 AM||||I'm missing how turbo 2.8's got factored into this equation. For those who don't know, ACE (the source of the hype initially posted at the start of the thread) does Supercharged 3800 swaps, not turbo kits. And yes, the 3.8 does fit nicely and provide sufficient acceleration for the Fiero. My opinion is that as powerplants become more high tech, it is going to be engines like the SBC that get left behind. Like it or not peoples, California-sized smog regulations will come to your town, and having a late-model clean running efficiant powerplant like the 3.8 will show its merits over the 350 chebby.  No flame intended here, I love the sound of a V8, but the 3.8 is a fine swap.||||reg||1
Z||000020||Slammed Fiero||01-03-2001||12:10 PM||||No the small block chevy isn't the greatest thing since sliced bread motor wise.<P>ponder this<P>In an average F1 engine, running at 18,000 rpm (300 revs per<BR>second), each spark plug fires 30 times a second. An n/a 3.0lt  V-10 F1<BR>engine produces about 280 hp/L on 97-octane unleaded pump gas. <P>A Dodge Viper 8.0L V-10 produces only 56 hp/L and an IRL 3.5L V-8 about 185 hp/L<BR>on methanol.<BR>    At 17 or 18000 RPM those pistons change direction about 500 times<BR>per second.  They said the wrist pins undergo somewhere around 8000 Gs<BR>of force at that speed.<P>ZO6 corvette , the best small block thus far has 67.54hp per liter , comapre that to a  Naturally aspirated VTec motor that curns out 240hp! from a tiny little 4cyl. Now what if Honda made the S2000 motor into a v8 ..4.0ltrs and 480hp , revs to 9000 rpm . sorry small block. (just a little what if)<P>thats 240hp from 2.0ltrs..or 120hp per liter or 52.46 more HP per liter than the allmighty Z06<P><BR> Sure it isn't the usual american HP with a big wall of torque , but the engineering that went into the motor is astounding! 3 cam shaft lobes for 2 valves , 2 for low end one for high end power..amazing! This creates a larger power curve. Sure it doesn't start til 400rpm , but it will scream all the way to 9000rpm , ask fierolisa her dad has an S2000. Now people may be juvinille and call me a honda lover ..but with engineering like that if you don't appreciate the motor your just ignorant.<P>There are tons of great small blocks powering cars to records. I doubt the research on the small block has been as on going as you would like to think. GM has a real progression problem when it comes to design , this coming from a person who has worked for them in marketing. <P>There are motors far more impressive than the Small block chevy.  There are motors that produce vastly more efficent HP. Sure spouting out some #'s about cars that do this that and the other thing with a small block is great. You talk about callaway's sledge hammer , Callaway a man who pioneered a lot of Turbo racing. I'm not sure isn't the sledge hammer one of those turbo's you hate???<P>The one lap of america has been one the last 2 years by a Porsche 911 , air cooled flat 6 turbo!!! 3.6ltrs!!! I get to hear all about it as I know 4 guys who run it successfully. Dan corcran , marc guimont , rick potter , steve cole.<P><BR>The ultima GTR is not AWD, True it's impressive  , but it's not a ground breaking design ... light car lotsa power...caroll shelby ..please stand up. <P>Now to cover my ass..... Dollar for Dollar you can't beat a small block and a ZZ4 is the plan for my fiero. I would love a Zr1motor , but financially it is out of reach. Yes true for no brainer HP the small block is great. it's plentiful , but even it has it's downfalls.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...||||reg||1
Z||000021||Slammed Fiero||01-03-2001||12:14 PM||||forgot to mention 300hp from a Vtec is quite easy.. I don't know what the current NSX is at HP wise , it is a 6 , but still a vtec motor , mosty independant dyno tests of the s2000 show it has 260 hp , up 20 from manufacturers tests. (after 5000 miles) <P>more to ponder..this thread should change it's name to ... the small block ..the BEST fiero alternative... <P>Jonathan McCreery||||reg||1
Z||000022||Philphine||01-03-2001||12:17 PM||||this is kind of why i've been watching the caddy 4.9 discussions and trying to get a look at all the sites mentioned (that place in indianapolis seems interesting). it looks like a good v8 compromise from a cost, weight, size (displacement and engine bay wise), and doability by a beginner like me wise. still waiting on the packet from bubbajoe and hoping the various people working on this swap keep us informed as much as possible. ||||reg||1
Z||000023||Verticalspeed||01-03-2001||12:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>Vertical, I clearly stated that it was just IMHO that is seemed crammed in there.  Somewhere on here I remember a V8 owner said there was an inch between the trunk and the V8 valve cover.  That sounds pretty crammed to me, thats where I based that opinion.  Again, that is an OPINION of mine, not a fact.  <P>I totally agree with Jonathan's post.  If I were going for reliability, a SBC would be it any day over a turbocharger.  BUT, in a Fiero that V8 looks a little crammed so I would imagine that if in the event something would go wrong it would be tough to fix,  then again some people say that its crammed with a V6 in there so I guess thats a moot point.<P>Also vertical, email Steve Hamm about his turbo setup, thats where I got those numbers from.  He is selling it now for only 2500.  <BR>Where did you get those V8 conversion numbers from?  What kind of motor are you getting for 1,000 bucks (305 = 180 Hp?)?  I have heard that while you can try to piece together Archie's kit for cheaper, it's such a pain in the butt that its better to just buy the kit.<P>well gotta run now I have to edit this later<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Motor from Checker auto parts: $800.00 300hp, flat top piston, RV cam.<BR>Conversion kit from archie: $950.00<P>The only part that's crammed is the right side of the engine which sits close against the fiero firewall. working on the motor is as simple as loosening the cradle bolts, removing he water pump and a few hoses and dropping the rear of the cradle or the whole thing. A 30 minute job if you've done it before.<BR>You can get a 300hp Vtech engine but at a much higher cost than a 300hp SBC. Anyday.<BR>Why is this guy selling his turbo for only $2,500? Why hasn't it sold yet for that cheap?ttl<P>||||reg||1
Z||000024||Verticalspeed||01-03-2001||01:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> <BR>Now to cover my ass..... Dollar for Dollar you can't beat a small block and a ZZ4 is the plan for my fiero.  [/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>That's my point exactly. You can look at all the "powerful" small motors out there but you always come back to the good ol' SBC.<BR>So you just went around in circles.<BR>None of the motors you mentioned above can be had for $500-$1,000 even coming from a junkyard car!<BR>I must agree with you that there are more "high tech" motors. Mostly the japanese stuff. Some japanese civics do 8's in the 1/4<BR>with HIGHLY MODIFIED, VERY LIGHT WEIGHT, NITROUS, Midget drivers on a diet, etc. But most civics owned by most people don't even come close unless the owner has spent $20,000 in parts. Why so much. A friend runs a rotary rx7 and runs 11's. His cost? Over $40,000 into that little car! He's oriental and rich. had that been a different car, say a SBC with $40,000 into it??? Hmmm food for thought.<BR>Let's cover our asses and do a v8 conversion.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000025||Slammed Fiero||01-03-2001||05:09 PM||||Conversion kit $900 , yeah ..plus everything else you need. there is a reason he has a master build kit for sale for 3 grand.<P><BR>$800 for a 300hp chev....I wouldn't mine seeing some specs on it ... iron head motor??<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...||||reg||1
Z||000026||Got2manyfieros||01-03-2001||05:54 PM||||I have to say that I'm not really sure what the point of this thread is, it seems that "Verticalspeed" already knows everything about why the small block chevy is great, but here is my two cents anyway:<P>The sbc is a good bang for the buck conversion for the fiero.  But with the handling potential of the car, and the midengined layout of the car I would prefer an aluminum over head cam medium displacement V8 (dare I say with variable cam timing).  I think a higher red line with an nice broad powerband instead of a brutal bottom end (lets face it, MOST sbc/fiero combinations are not set up to rev) would create a sports car.<P>For my money, the sbc is a dinosaur.  It still has pushrods.  The head design is not wonderful, even though it can be made to produce big power.  And the distributer is in the back, so in any traditional rear wheel drive installation, it is more difficult to play with the timing.  GM got their moneys worth from this design, but they needed to let it go a little earlier.<P>Now comes the LS1 family.  I was excited to learn that GM was completely redesighning their V8.  I am thrilled that it is made of light weight metal alloys.  The six bolt cross bolted main bearing caps are great.  The head design is much improved as well with even exhaust port spacing.  IT STILL HAS PUSHRODS.  We thought they'd figure it out after the zr1.<P>Now that I am off of my soapbox:<P>I think the northstar would be ideal...but the aftermarket for it is far from complete.<P>I think the 3.4 OHC motor is probably the next best option given what is available from gm, cause it is not worth it to try and figure out how to put the 3.4L V8 dohc motor from a SHO taurus into a gm product.<P>the GM 3.4 has a decent aftermarket with it, aluminum heads, overhead cams, and it will bolt up easily.  I'm sure with some persuasion, a turbo could probably be adapted, that would be an EVIL motor easily 300+ horse with a 7000rpm redline, and no heavier than the stock 2.8<P>I understand that cost is a big issue, but there are a lot of ways to make big power in a fiero without using ancient technology.||||reg||1
Z||000027||Slammed Fiero||01-03-2001||07:56 PM||||could ya give me more insight into the 3.4???<BR>all I know is that it's 215hp , I know displacement and internals. What are the issues with the motor , performance ect?<P><BR>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000028||hugh||01-03-2001||08:10 PM||||It costs money to make power,no matter what engine you use.As far as the mouse motor is concerned,the original primitive model of this engine dates back to around 1917.When the 265 engine came out in 1955 it used many of the features of that 1917 prototype(pushrods,overhead valves,etc.)I have a 87GN that is 231ci and developes over 425hp thanks to modern technology.If I have a Chevy dinosaur or a high tech motor doesn't make any difference if I can blow the doors off most of the cars on the highway.The only advantage to the high tech part is I can get high gas mileage at the same time.One last thing.I saw an article in one of the auto mags a while back(Popular Hot Rod,I think)about a 1986/87 Buick regal.The guy drove a good distance to work every day and ran in the eight's at Englishtown(NJ) with a turbocharged smallblock. ||||reg||3
Z||000029||KissMySSFiero||01-03-2001||09:23 PM||||here's food for thought....i believe it costs stockcar racers somewhere in the ballpark of 30 grand to build their 600hp stock car motors.  someone said something about 40k for a motor.  well thats what you get.  600hp that kicks for hours on end.  damn my girlfriend would that.....<BR><P>------------------<BR>JOEJOE  88FORMULA<BR>KissMySSFiero@netzero.net<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FORUMSIG1.jpg">  <BR>"There's a fine line between love and beer"||||reg||1
Z||000030||rockcrawl||01-03-2001||09:39 PM||||Pushrods forever!!!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Comparing my Fords (yes I'll admit I drive Fords), a '97 5.4 Triton OHC, a '92 EFI 5.8 pushrod, a '86 EFI 5.0 pushrod all similar vehicles. The triton has the highest Hp rating of all and probably the highest torque, but the old pushrod engines will run circles around it. The damn thing doesn't make any power until it hits who knows what outrageosly high rpm (no tach). My point is, seat-of-the-pants feel overrules specs on paper.<P>When it comes to Fiero conversions I don't care about hp/liter, for me it's hp/dolar that's important, so forget the Hi-tech V-tech. Weight is another big consideration.<P>In the same car a 300hp SBC would probably blow a 300hp V-tech away and for 1/4 the cost. Maybe not? <P>No flames intended, I like all engines. I was just brought up on old american iron. Usually I say things like "If it's not powerfull enough, it's not big enough". <P>This really has turned into a useless thread!<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000031||Racer 1||01-04-2001||12:00 AM||||This guy in town had a 305 tucked in his fiero. It looked cool and all was fast but turning the fiero under power sucked. I would go with a Quad 4 HO myself. Why not leave in the stock motor and just enjoy driving your fiero. Plus the fiero only has a 8-10 gallon tank for a v8 hehe.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://spitfire.cwv.net/~cdbailey/images/gifssucl/eyes2/evil_eyes.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000032||Cheever2||01-04-2001||12:17 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>...300 revs per second, each spark plug fires 30 times a second...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Remember, it's a 4-stroke engine... the plugs fire <B>15</B> times a second.  But who's counting?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR><I>Chuck Hodges</I>||||reg||1
Z||000033||Matt88||01-04-2001||12:20 AM||||I have no idea what it takes to do a V8 conversion, however I do know something about Chevy small blocks.  Since the SBC produces good torque figures in the mid RPM range (3000-5000) Chevy was not interested in producing high HP to move their heavy cars and trucks.  <P>However, with little modification, it is easy to produce 300hp.  A manual written by David Vizard shows a SBC 350 from a wrecked car with 150,000 miles, with a quick cyl. honing and new rings (the old cast pistons were used) with a mild cam, cast iron intake and 4bbl carb produced over 300hp and 350ft/lb of torque.  His cost in parts and labor was $644 in 1998.<P>Getting 300hp and 350ft/lbs of torque out of a 2.8 would cost much more than $650.<P>But, if I wanted a SBC 350, I would have bought an old Camaro - they're a lot cheaper, but nowhere as unique as my '88 GT!||||reg||1
Z||000034||Verticalspeed||01-04-2001||02:51 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>Conversion kit $900 , yeah ..plus everything else you need. there is a reason he has a master build kit for sale for 3 grand.<BR>$800 for a 300hp chev....I wouldn't mine seeing some specs on it ... iron head motor??<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Man this is pathetic. <BR>O.k. if you want to add to the $900.00<P>$275 for water pump drive<BR>You do not need the $350 4-core radiator unless you live in very hot climate.<P>Let me share something with you that you should already know: when you go to any local race track on drag nights, who's running the best times with less money? Hondas,nissans, civics or fords and chevy's?<BR>1/4 mile times rule on the roads because most of the useful enjoyable speeds are from quick runs.<BR>The SBC is a good for torque where it matters the most.<BR>FYI I have done my own v8 conversion for less tha $3,000 and I am proud of the results. I don't need to prove anything to you in terms of the specs on my engine. But bring your honda, Vtech, fart can pipe import and race me at the 1/4 mile and you will be happy to watch my tailights in the distance. With upgraded suspension components we could even do the high speed thing. AND, <B>get this</B>: there are guys here on the forum with MUCH BETTER engines than mine, 383 strokers and all that would make <B>me</B> watch their tailights.<BR>[b]never underestimate the power to weight/gear ratios and the power of the good ol' SBC. Especially in modified form. <BR>||||reg||6
Z||000035||Will||01-04-2001||10:20 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cheever2:<BR><B> [QUOTE]Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR>[b]...300 revs per second, each spark plug fires 30 times a second...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE>Remember, it's a 4-stroke engine... the plugs fire <B>15</B> times a second.  But who's counting?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>[/B][/QUOTE]<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> At 300 revolutions per second a spark plug fires <I>150</I> times per second.||||reg||1
Z||000036||Will||01-04-2001||10:44 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>Conversion kit $900 , yeah ..plus everything else you need. there is a reason he has a master build kit for sale for 3 grand.<P>$800 for a 300hp chev....I wouldn't mine seeing some specs on it ... iron head motor??<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Nothing's wrong with iron cylinder heads. In fact, because iron has a lower heat conductivity than aluminum, iron will take less heat away from combustion and promote more power (compared to an <I>identical</I> aluminum cylinder head.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000037||Will||01-04-2001||11:03 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B>No the small block chevy isn't the greatest thing since sliced bread motor wise.<P>&lt;snip&gt;<P>ZO6 corvette , the best small block thus far has 67.54hp per liter , comapre that to a  Naturally aspirated VTec motor that curns out 240hp! from a tiny little 4cyl. Now what if Honda made the S2000 motor into a v8 ..4.0ltrs and 480hp , revs to 9000 rpm . sorry small block. (just a little what if)<P>thats 240hp from 2.0ltrs..or 120hp per liter or 52.46 more HP per liter than the allmighty Z06<P><BR> Sure it isn't the usual american HP with a big wall of torque , but the engineering that went into the motor is astounding! 3 cam shaft lobes for 2 valves , 2 for low end one for high end power..amazing! This creates a larger power curve. Sure it doesn't start til 400rpm , but it will scream all the way to 9000rpm , ask fierolisa her dad has an S2000. Now people may be juvinille and call me a honda lover ..but with engineering like that if you don't appreciate the motor your just ignorant.<BR>&lt;snip&gt;<BR>Now to cover my ass..... Dollar for Dollar you can't beat a small block and a ZZ4 is the plan for my fiero. I would love a Zr1motor , but financially it is out of reach. Yes true for no brainer HP the small block is great. it's plentiful , but even it has it's downfalls.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>There seems to be some animosity toward pushrod engines, and a perception of them as being "low tech".<BR>Ponder this: The ZO6 Corvette competes with the best sports cars in the world, but costs at most a third as much. Road and Track (?) recently did an article in which, both hot lapped by a professional driver, the Corvette was less than 1 second behind the Ferrari 360, considered by many the consumate sports car of our time. The Corvette was also less than 1 second <I>ahead</I> of the Ferrari 550. The 360 costs 3 1/2 times what the Corvette costs and the 550 costs over 5 times what the Corvette costs.<P>     The LS6 (ZO6 engine) uses magnetic crank shaft trigger and DIS. In the rare event the engine pings, the computer can determine the individual cylinder which is pinging and retard the timing for that cylinder only. It uses coil on plug ignition. The injector spray patterns are such that the fuel never touches the walls of the intake runner or the intake valve, but goes directly into the combustion chamber. The computer stays in closed loop operation at wide open throttle. In spite of all this, most of what I hear are complaints that it uses pushrods.<P>     There is a reason the ZR1 is no longer being produced. The program wasn't worth the time and money! The LT5 (ZR1 engine) costs 25,000 dollars new. Currently the LS6 is every bit as powerful (on paper, anyway) as the LT5. The only thing the LT5 has on the LS6 is durability (The LT5 can take more punishment than most anvils). The LT5 electronics are primitive compared to the LS6 electronics.<P>     The Northstar, hailed as GM's "high tech engine", produces a tick over 65 hp/litre. The LS6 produces over 67 hp/litre, pushrods and all.<P>     120 HP/litre is not that impressive from an engine that revs to 9000 rpm. An SBC that revved to 9k would produce similar specific output. The SBC just would not be anywhere close to tractable enough for the street. How many S2000's that are driven anywhere close to their potential are going to last 100K miles?<P>I will be far more impressed with Honda engines when they can produce 100 ftlbs/litre naturally aspirated at reasonable engine speeds. <P>     Why does the Corvette get better gas mileage than the S2000, with pushrods and three times the displacement?<P>You don't think engineering goes into the small block? Maybe they're trying to increase power yet stay at reasonable engine speeds. Do you know what that requires? It requires TORQUE. How much do you know about fluid flow dynamics? Could you design a cylind head that makes more torque than the LS6 cylinder head? Conversely, how much engineering know how, comparatively, does it take to slap solid lifters and a huge cam in an engine and rev it to the moon?<BR>     I guess you don't read GM High Tech Performance magazine. They recently featured an article on the LS6. There is an enormous amount of engineering know-how going into that engine.<BR>     I am corresponding with a gentleman who put $5k into his B18C5 ITR engine. It has detonation problems on 91 octane gas with 10.6:1 compression. The L99 ('94 Caprice) engine that my father has in his 280 Z has 10:1 compression and runs beautifully on '87 octane. It also pulls from 25 mph is fifth. With pushrods. How many stock Hondas do that?<P>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 01-04-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Will (edited 01-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000038||Verticalspeed||01-04-2001||01:04 PM|||| <BR>When you buy a ferrari, you're buying a name and <B>excellent</B> handling, but you are not buying horse power and low end speed.<BR>Ferrari's and other exotic cars are notoriously famous for autobahn high speed driving. they are also heavy cars. The testarossa weighs over 4,000 lbs with driver, probably more.<BR><B>Most exotic cars are elusive!</B>.<BR>The new corvette just proved that with 385hp in stock form. Imagine adding some mods to it!<BR>Back to the fiero and chevy subject. By the way, have you noticed that when people are losing ground in their arguments, they start saying this is a "useless thread"?<BR>Putting a v8 into a small car is nothing new.<BR>I have seen Porsches 911 and even miatas with v8 engines in them. The advantage of the v8 is in its price and simplicity and what it does for a little car like the fiero.<BR>[b]I must admit, though, that handling at high speeds is kind of scary with the fiero's stock suspension. The front end seems to be too light unless you put a floor jack in there like I did and the car definitely doesn't feel as tight as a ferrari for example at 90-120MPH.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000039||Black88GT||01-04-2001||04:28 PM||||I have seen the V8s in Miata's too.  I think some guy in California does it?  Not sure what V8 he uses though.<P>I think the whole V8 thing comes down to personal preference.  If your an old schooler with that American muscle car upbringing, I can see how you would want to stuff a SBC in anything with 4 wheels.  On the other hand, if you are from the newer generation you are looking toward different technology, i.e. why 95% of the Honda guys are in their 20s or younger.<P>For the record, I know of a v-tec CRX that runs low 13s N/A, but thats with slicks.  Thats pretty impressive from a small motor.  With a small shot of NOS he can break into the 12s.  He has his laptop hooked up to it and everything, its pretty cool.  I would like to see some sub 13 second timeslips from a SBC Fiero.<P>Ferrari's are the best of both worlds.  Lots of displacement, mid engined, rev like a vtec motor will, run circles around everything and as fast as anything.  I guess thats why they are way to expensive for us and we drive Fiero's...<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000040||rockcrawl||01-04-2001||04:39 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>have you noticed that when people are losing ground in their arguments, they start saying this is a "useless thread"?<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>It's a useless thread because nobody will ever win the argument. It has gotten pretty far off of the original topic. Nobody is comparing apples to apples. Some people are talking about race engines that aren't streetable nor affordable. Everyone has their own opinion of what is good or bad. Some people think a 300hp v6 is better than a 300hp v8 and vice-versa. The only good thing coming out of it is possibly some gained knowledge about engine technology, and some good fun healthy arguing!<P>It's kinda like a Ford vs Chevy thread, it's never gonna go anywhere. No offense implied to anyone.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000041||Will||01-04-2001||05:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR><B> It's a useless thread because nobody will ever win the argument. It has gotten pretty far off of the original topic. Nobody is comparing apples to apples. Some people are talking about race engines that aren't streetable nor affordable. Everyone has their own opinion of what is good or bad. Some people think a 300hp v6 is better than a 300hp v8 and vice-versa. The only good thing coming out of it is possibly some gained knowledge about engine technology, and some good fun healthy arguing!<P>It's kinda like a Ford vs Chevy thread, it's never gonna go anywhere. No offense implied to anyone.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't think it's useless, but I don't think anyone will ever "win" either, even though I'm right.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>PULEASE don't spout off that "it's off the original topic" crap. The original topic didn't have much to offer. People finished with it and are now "discussing" things they find more interesting. If you have something to add to the original topic, by all means add it. If you don't have anything to add except rain on the parade, DON'T. If you don't like this thread, the next is a mouse-click away. Some of us happen to like arguing and will be perfectly happy to debate unprovable points, but we'll be happier when no one is telling us "this is useless".<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000042||Tina||01-04-2001||07:16 PM||||Wow, you guys are STILL at it!<BR>While I do agree the SBC is not the greatest things since sliced bread,and perhaps is <BR>a dinosaur compared to what is on the market <BR>today.<BR>It works for me.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B> would you rather listen to, the high-pitched whine of a 4/6-cyl turbo or the rumble of a V-8 exhaust and the sound of a 4bbl carb sucking 850cfm of air!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Tina<BR>84 coupe with a 383 and loving it||||reg||1
