Z||000046||Verticalspeed||01-04-2001||09:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B>KILL this Thread???????<P> <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><B>Hey, Archie, it's me again...always adding coal to the fire! I got my B.S. degree in topic creation... Now that's bachelors of science not bull ****  degree.<BR></B><BR>Anyway here's what I like:<P>High torque/hp V-8's<BR>High torque/hp v-10s' (viper)<BR>High torque/hp v-12's (ferrari)<BR>But not surprisingly, the v8 allows us to beat the v-10's and v-12's for a lot less money than it takes a v-10 to produce 600hp (Hennessey viper) or a turbo charged v-12 testarossa (500hp or so)<BR>So...<B>Archie</B>, tell us some stories about some of your best v8 cars and perhaps some time slips for the record!<BR>I think you know who I am but let's keep my identity secret. I enjoy talking to all these fine guys here. I almost feel like part of a big happy family, although I am not!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000047||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||01-04-2001||10:35 PM||||Seems like it may be time for a sanity check.<BR>How many horsepower do you need for a Fiero? Do you need one horsepower for every 10 pounds?  <BR>A 2.8L is good for say 140 hp in stock form. In a 2800lb car that is 1 horsie for every 20 pounds.  In a 2.5L fiero it is more like 27 pounds per pony.<BR>If you had 200 hp in your Fiero, that would be just about a 50% increase in power.  If you were doing 0 to 60 in less than 8 seconds, you should be getting into 6 second range.  Isn't that enough??  <BR>If you get to 60 in less than 6 seconds and then let up on the gas, how fast are you going when the car stops accelerating?  <BR>As I read this thread, I began to question the sanity of the V8 swap.  My experiences driving in the Sillicon Valley make clear that a 4 cylinder Fiero is plenty to dart around in traffic.  If you are a canyon carver, then the 2.8L V6 should be capable of accelerating strongly up any hill.  <BR>A sports car is about handling.  A supercar is about handling and big power.  Sports cars are fun, supercars are about responsibility and self control on US roads.  <BR>Hey!  Your Fiero is not a supercar.  You can make it one, but for what?  If you want to race then buy a real racing car.  If you want to brag, then understand that you are ruining a very fun little car to talk big.  What is the point if you have to drive your car carefully all the time.  <BR>I beleive the reason there are no V8 timeslips posted is because seriously trying to drag race a Fiero V8 can lead to serious problems.  From this perspective, the 2.8 and 3.4L Fieros are a lot more fun.  And, I fully understand the motive for an HO Quad 4 swap.  A Fiero that revs past 5,500 rpm sounds like a blast.  <BR>If you have to win every race from stoplight to stoplight, then buy a Mustang GT.  Yes, it is an awefull car to drive in the rain.  But, you can have as much power as your wallet will cover. That's what Mustangs are all about.  There are so many of them out there that a huge aftermarket industry is feasible.  Given the less than 350,000 every made, this will never be so with the Fiero.   <BR>Leave your Fiero with it's stock powerplant for those times when you drive for your own pleasure. Fieros are a lot more fun to drive than a Mustang or Camaro.  That's why we are all here at this forum, remember?||||reg||3
Z||000048||Black88GT||01-04-2001||11:41 PM||||I think you opened up a big can of worms with that post.  <P>This thread is kind of like a Chevy v. Ford thread, but hey its all in fun so who cares..<P>To response the "Lucky's" thread.  I think almost everyone who has posted here can agree the Fiero was underpowered from the factory.  That's a given.  I mean any car that is labeled a "sports" car IMHO should have sub 16 second qtr. mile times and decent 0-60, especially when its setup like an exotic car like the Fiero.  I guess with Fiero's it just breaks down to who wants to go all the way with the power and who wants to mildly mod.  Most people that want to go all the way go with V8's, others don't.  The main reason I did my 3.4 was not for speed so much as necessity.  My 2.8 died and it would be dumb IMHO to replace it with another 2.8, and when your on a very tight budget and time limit, the 3.4 is the way to go.  If I would have had more time I probably would have gotten a 3.8.  If I would have had more time and money I would have went with possibly a V8 or a built up 3.8 SC.  <P>In a way I can see your viewpoint, I even agree to a certain extent.  I think we should leave the V8 to the f-bodies/mustangs, but I think it was vertical speed who made the comment about V8's being in a lot of small cars and he has a point.  Like I said in my last post, it all breaks down to personal preference...<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
Z||000049||Sillhouette.com||01-04-2001||11:42 PM||||Holy That's interesting!<P>Took a while to read all this it's quite interesting. <P>The 3.8sc swap well it's been done nothing new there the performance is not all that impressive either.  My last experience with a 3.8SC was with a car whoes owner spent over 60,000 dollars on it.  Including tubular A arms extrude hone supercharger custom pistons raised comperssion digital gauges and NOS.  Still it only ran 14.1s the NOS was never configured properly something was a miss with that engine.  <P>V8 swaps again nothing new there.  The fascination with the V8 is the mental picture of fitting one of those into such a small car.  The performance gains are automatically assumed.  So immagine my superise when a V8 fiero with carburator stalled on the Solo track at Barrie.  It seemed impressive when the engine did not bog down or stall.  Even when it did not stall it got beaten by CRX and other non V8 Fieros.  This might be driver related though... <BR>The lesson from this is when you beat a V8 with OHH lets say a supercharged 2.8 it's a satisfaction on it's own.  The look on a loosers face when he figures out that it's only a V6 that smoked him is priceless.  <P>Now turbos.  There is only ONE turbo that impressed me. The Chris West mod the craftsmanship and detail to performance is exceptional.  I can't immagine picking up a turbo from some wrecked car and piping it into an engine as being safe usefull or cost effective.  The turbos that I have experience with are one from RACETECH and one from Design one.  I think that Racetech actually stole the Design one system but that's another story.  They claim 12 s in the 1/4 mile is false.  It's more like 15s in the 1/4 mile.  And to add to insult both of the turbos broke down and had to be towed back from the Niagara bridge to Toronto.  Something with too much heat and coolant lines exploding and flowing backwards through the exhaust valves.  The engine was destroyed.  <P>So my point of all this is do what is your fancy. If you want V8 get a V8 if you want Turbo get a turbo if you want 3.8 SC get it.  The interesting part however is that you can get pretty much the same perfomance number from a properly modified 2.8 as other engine mods.  So what's the point in doing the SWAP?  <P>Cheers<BR>13.4s SC 2.8 with a passanger show tires full tank , stereo and camping equipment.  Oh yeah sunglasses and open windows.   ||||reg||1
Z||000050||Slammed Fiero||01-05-2001||01:02 AM||||That 14.1 sec NOS 3.8 ....it isn'ty burgandy is it??? There is a guy up here in canada that claimed up and down 11's for his car , told stories about how fast it was and ran a low 14 in the 1/4.<P>I have heard the racetech turbo car runs low 13's on a supposedly internally stock 2.8 with only 10 psi boost. But how believable that is I'm not sure. I have heard from a good friend that the car does run low 13's at the strip. But here say is here say. I want video's , slips ect.<P>the motor you said racetech destroyed was from an oil line being looped , they managed to take the turbo out of the loop in the process and it fried there turbo. I hear big #'s from a lot of these fiero "wanna be" racers but never see them race. They have all the tricks on there car , talk about beating vipers and all this , but we never see actual proof. <P>you mention a V8 stall in barrie , you mean barrie Ontario??  For me a V8 is more a facsimile of a Pantera , a Mclaren from a the 60/70's Can am car  ect. Mid engine V8 that I can afford to build and Afford to work on with basic tools. <P>for some people stock is the way to go, and for somethings I agree.. But a Fiero was born to be tinkered with . It's really the last true American hot rod. built just before all the techno stuff took a lot of the fun out of hot rodding.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan M McCreery<BR>86 GT<BR>New Custom White face gauges 17" Tr motorsports Typhoons Ground Control coil overs , Big bars front and rear , Mustang scoop , accel coil , no cat , 4spd , short shift , poly dog bone and bushings momo pedals and shift knob , round headlight conversion , Geni stainless steel oval exhaust tips. Pioneer 6000 CD player , Sony Mobile Amp. Currently doing big brake conversion...||||reg||1
